# Line endings.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, which checks
# files out of git with CRLF endings. That is fine for source we only ever
# compile — and fatal for anything Linux executes directly.
#
# A `#!/bin/sh\r` shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter literally
# named "/bin/sh\r", and the container dies with the maximally unhelpful
# `exec /app/docker-entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory` on a file that
# plainly exists. That took production down during the 2026-08-09 deploy: the
# release was shipped as a tar of the Windows working tree, so every CRLF came
# with it and the backend crash-looped.
#
# Forcing LF on the files the container executes or parses removes the whole
# class of failure at the source, rather than relying on remembering to convert
# on the way out.

# Let git decide for ordinary text, normalising to LF in the repository.
* text=auto

# MUST be LF — Linux executes or parses these directly.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*    text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
docker-compose*.yml  text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Windows-only tooling stays CRLF.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.apk binary
*.aab binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.sqlite3 binary
